raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Does substance use play a weaker role in whom adolescents nominate, who gets nominated, and whether friends have similar use histories in American Indian (AI)–majority reservation schools than prior research suggests for racially heterogeneous schools?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Consequently, we expect that substance use will show weaker sender,\nreceiver, and homophily effects in AI-majority schools than observed in heterogeneous populations.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do reservation-school friendships show the familiar patterns of reciprocating nominations and befriending friends of friends, and might those patterns be especially strong in close-knit communities?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This cultural foundation translates the structural tendency for\nreciprocity into a normative expectation within social conduct, suggesting that reciprocal friendships may be especially\npronounced in AI adolescent networks [4].",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we expect this tendency toward closure to be at least as strong as—and plausibly\nstronger than—that documented in less cohesive school settings, given the dense overlap of familial, school, and communal social spheres.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do the distributions of friendship nominations suggest that popular students attract disproportionately many ties, or are they more consistent with egalitarian constraints on popularity?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We therefore explore the nature of degree effects, whether they indicate preferential\nattachment or egalitarian constraints, within these specific AI adolescent contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Is depression associated with receiving fewer nominations, sending fewer nominations through withdrawal, or forming ties with peers who have similar symptoms?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We\ntherefore examine which of these mechanisms—receiver effects, withdrawal-related sender effects, or homophily—\noperate in AI-majority school contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does the strength of AI identity affirmation relate to sending or receiving friendship nominations or to choosing friends with similar levels of affirmation? The authors explore these possibilities without predicting a direction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Alternatively, because AI identity is largely shared in\nthis population, its predictive power for tie formation may be limited. Given the exploratory nature of this investigation, we\ndo not formulate directional hypotheses but examine sender, receiver, and homophily effects empirically.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do the familiar patterns of same-gender and same-grade friendship recur in AI-majority schools, and does shared sexual orientation also structure friendships?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Substantively, gender and grade homophily are among the most robust findings in adolescent network research [9, 12]; we expect\nthese patterns to replicate in AI-majority schools, where gender- and grade-segregated activities similarly organize peer\ninteraction.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual orientation homophily has received limited attention in the literature, but documented social stratification along sexual identity lines in adolescent populations suggests\nthat it may likewise structure friendship ties [9].",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "In the three AI-majority reservation schools, friendship networks were organized primarily by familiar network processes and shared demographic characteristics, not by substance use. Depression and AI identity affirmation also contributed little to explaining ties.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These findings reveal a distinct\nrelational ecology within AI-majority reservation schools, where peer ties are organized around structural and demographic commonalities rather than risky behaviors.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "Similarly, depression and AI identity were not\nsubstantively associated with friendship formation.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors argue that network-based substance-use prevention should be tailored to local school relationships and should explore strengths that bring AI youth together rather than assume that risky behavior is what connects them.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This heterogeneity carries practical implications: network-based interventions for substance use prevention may need to be calibrated to\nschool-specific relational ecologies rather than applied uniformly across reservation settings.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Gaining insight into these alternative predictors is\ncrucial for developing innovative, effective strategies for substance use prevention tailored to AI youth that may center on\nstrengths-based connections rather than social ties rooted in risky behavior.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors conducted a cross-sectional survey of ninth- and tenth-graders in three purposively selected schools on one Northern Plains reservation: two tribal schools in different villages and a private college-preparatory school.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In our study, we established partnerships with three schools on a large Northern Plains reservation to conduct a comprehensive cross-sectional social network survey among 9th- and 10th-grade students.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The schools were purposefully\nselected to represent a broad spectrum of social environments on the reservation.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Our selection included a tribal school\nin one of the most geographically isolated and smallest villages (school A), another larger tribal school in a more populous\nvillage (school B), and a private, college preparatory school situated near the largest village in the area (school C).",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey obtained 263 responses, a 77% overall response rate. The network analysis used 247 students with complete network data: 49 in School A, 128 in School B, and 70 in School C.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We\nhad an overall response rate of 77% across the three schools. Specifically, 72% of eligible students completed surveys in\nSchool A (n = 52 out of 72), 76% in School B (n = 140 out of 184), and 84% in School C (n = 71 out of 84), for a total sample\nsize of 263.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the 247 participants with complete network data, item non-response was limited to the substance use variables.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "School A (n = 49) was the smallest, located in\na geographically isolated village; School B (n = 128) was the largest, serving a more populous community; and School C\n(n = 70) was a private college-preparatory institution.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Students completed a two-part Network Canvas survey on tablets during school hours. Community and youth advisory boards reviewed the measures for cultural appropriateness and relevance.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey consisted of two primary sections: the first collected demographic information, measures of AI identity, depression, and substance use; the second collected directed friendship nominations. We used Network Canvas, a software\nplatform designed for collecting social network data [32].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "All measures were reviewed for cultural appropriateness and\nrelevance by a Community Advisory Board and a Youth Advisory Board from the participating communities prior to finalization [33].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Trained research team members administered the survey during school hours. Students provided responses directly on\ntablets.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The outcome was a directed friendship network for each school. Students could nominate up to ten close friends in grades 7–12 using school rosters, but the analysis kept only ties among responding ninth- and tenth-graders.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The dependent variable was the directed friendship network within each school. Students were\nasked to “select up to 10 friends you are closest with in grades 7th-12th at your school.”",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "School rosters within the Network\nCanvas software assisted identification. For analysis, we focused on ties among responding 9th- and 10th-grade students,\nstored in a square adjacency matrix for each school.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Alcohol, tobacco, and marijuana were measured separately as binary self-reports of ever using each substance. The cigarette question excluded ceremonial tobacco use.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants’ use of alcohol, tobacco, and marijuana was\nmeasured through binary variables (yes = 1; no = 0), derived from self-reported data in the survey’s initial section.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Have you ever had a drink (more than a sip) of alcohol, such as beer,\nwine, or hard liquor like vodka?”, “Have you ever smoked a cigarette? (Do not include ceremonial use of tobacco.)”, and\n“Have you ever used marijuana (also called weed, pot, chronic, or nug, including edibles)?”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For substance-use refusals, the authors used reports from peers who nominated the respondent. They classified a respondent as a user if more than half of those peers reported use at least a few times in the past year, and as a non-user otherwise.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, 16 participants (6.5%) selected ‘Refuse to answer’ for alcohol use, 14 (5.7%) for tobacco use, and 21 (8.5%)\nfor marijuana use. For these cases, we utilized available peer perception data to infer behavior.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "If over half of a respondent’s incoming friendship nominations (i.e., peers who had nominated them as a friend)\nindicated peer perception of them engaging in a specific substance use behavior (response ≥ 2, corresponding to ‘a few\ntimes a year’ or more), the respondent was classified as a user (1) for that variable;",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "otherwise, they were classified as a\nnon-user (0).",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Depressive symptoms were measured with six CES-D items, each rated from 0 for rare symptoms to 3 for symptoms occurring most or all of the time.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey also included a measure of depression, utilizing the Center for Epidemiologic\nStudies Depression Scale (CES-D) [35–38]. This section of the survey consisted of six questions, each probing different\nfacets of depression, such as persistent sadness, feelings of failure, loneliness, and the occurrence of crying spells.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Respondents were asked to indicate the frequency of these symptoms using a scale ranging from 0 (rarely) to 3 (most or\nall of the time).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "AI identity affirmation was the average of six adapted Multigroup Ethnic Identity Measure items about belonging, attachment, and pride. The wording was tailored to the participating Tribe with the advisory boards.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The measurement of AI identity was assessed using six items adapted from the Affirmation and Belonging\nsubscale of the Multigroup Ethnic Identity Measure (MEIM) [39].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "For cultural relevance and specificity within this tribal community,\nitem wording was adapted in partnership with the study’s Community and Youth Advisory Boards, with generic references\nto “TRIBE” replaced by the specific tribal identifier.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "An\naverage score across these six items was computed to represent overall AI identity affirmation. The adapted scale demonstrated good internal consistency in the present sample (Cronbach’s α = 0.722).",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Grade came from school rosters. Survey responses were grouped as male, female, or other for gender and as straight or non-straight for sexual orientation. Demographics served both as homophily benchmarks and as adjustment variables for the focal attributes.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The information about each respondent’s grade level was obtained based on school rosters\nprovided by the participating schools.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "we processed gender information by male (if male is one of the selected options), female\n(if female is one of the selected options), and other (if male and female are not in the selected options).",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Given\nthe low prevalence of responses outside of the straight category, and to simplify the model, we categorized sexual\norientation as straight and non-straight.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "the homophily terms capture substantively meaningful patterns of friendship formation along demographic lines, which we report and interpret as\nreplication benchmarks.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "the demographic sender and receiver terms additionally",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "serve as adjustment variables that account for demographic sorting in friendship nominations, so that the focal attribute\neffects can be interpreted net of these compositional forces.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The observed networks were sparse to moderately dense, with high reciprocity and moderate clustering. School C had the highest density and average number of nominations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Network densities ranged from 0.026 (School\nB) to 0.083 (School C), reflecting the low-to-moderate tie density typical of friendship nomination networks where students select from a restricted roster. Reciprocity rates were high across all schools (93.3–97.7%), indicating that mutual\nfriendship nominations were the norm.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Transitivity\nlevels ranged from 31.0% (School B) to 36.5% (School A), suggesting moderate clustering. Notably, School C showed the\nhighest density and mean degree despite having a mid-sized student body",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors fitted a school-specific exponential random graph model (ERGM) to account for dependence among ties. Structural terms represented reciprocity, triadic closure, incoming and outgoing degree distributions, and open two-step paths so that closure could be distinguished from the availability of those paths.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We employed Exponential Random Graph Models (ERGMs) to model the probability of friendship tie formation within\neach school, accounting for the inherent interdependence of network observations [9,24].",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "We included standard parameters to account for structural network dependence: reciprocity\n(mutual ties), transitivity (modeled via the geometrically weighted edgewise shared partner, GWESP, term), and the\ndistribution of incoming and outgoing ties (modeled via geometrically weighted indegree, GWIDeg, and outdegree,\nGWODeg, terms) [23,24].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Following Snijders et al. [28], we also included a two‑path term to separate the tendency for\nopen two‑step sequences from triadic closure, thereby allowing the GWESP coefficient to more cleanly capture transitivity.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Each school had a base model with network structure, demographics, depression, and AI identity, plus three separate models adding sender, receiver, and homophily terms for one substance at a time.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For each school, we estimated a series of ERGMs. This included a base model containing structural terms (reciprocity, transitivity [GWESP], degree distributions [GWIDeg, GWODeg], and a two-path term) and core substantive variables\n(demographics, depression, AI identity).",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Subsequently, we estimated separate models for each substance (alcohol,\ntobacco, marijuana) that added sender (nodeofactor), receiver (nodeifactor), and homophily (nodematch) effects for the\nspecific substance use variable.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The models were estimated by Markov chain Monte Carlo maximum likelihood. The authors used average marginal effects to compare changes in predicted tie probability across schools rather than compare the raw ERGM coefficients.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Parameters were estimated using Markov chain\nMonte Carlo (MCMC) maximum likelihood estimation via the ergm package in R [42].",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "A key challenge in interpreting and comparing ERGM coefficients across different models or contexts is their non-linear\nscaling, making direct comparison of θ coefficients misleading [43].",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "AMEs represent\nthe average change in the predicted probability of a tie formation associated with a one-unit change in a predictor variable, holding all other variables constant.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "All 12 primary models converged without detected degeneracy. Goodness-of-fit checks compared observed network statistics with those from 100 simulated networks per model and indicated adequate fit.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "all 12 models converged successfully within 25 iterations.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Model degeneracy was assessed by inspecting coefficient estimates for extreme values and MCMC samples\nfor non-finite statistics; no degeneracy was detected in any specification.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Goodness-of-fit was assessed by comparing the observed network to 100 networks simulated from each fitted model\n(Figs S1–S12 in S2 File): on degree distributions (indegree and outdegree), edgewise and dyadwise shared partner distributions, geodesic distances, and the triad census.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "In all 12 models, the observed network statistics fell within the central\nrange of simulated distributions, indicating adequate model fit.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "To check whether adjustment for other student characteristics masked substance-use associations, the authors re-estimated models containing only the structural and substance-use terms. The lack of a consistent substance-use pattern persisted.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To assess whether the absence of substance‑use effects might be due to overcontrol (i.e., the inclusion of depression, AI identity, and demographic covariates), we estimated separate ERGMs containing only structural terms and the\nsubstance use variables (alcohol, tobacco, marijuana).",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "substance use showed no robust or consistent association with sender, receiver, or\nhomophily effects across schools, while the structural processes (reciprocity, transitivity, degree effects) remained significant.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Reciprocity and triadic closure were consistently strong across the three schools: students tended to return nominations and to nominate friends of friends, while open two-step paths had negative estimates.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Reciprocity was positive and highly significant in every model, indicating a robust tendency for mutual friendship nominations.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Similarly, the positive coefficients for closed triads (transitivity) and negative coefficients for two‑path confirmed that triadic\nclosure—the process by which friends of friends become friends—is a fundamental organizing principle within these AI\nadolescent networks.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "In all three schools, a subset of students sent disproportionately many nominations. Only the private preparatory school, School C, showed a statistically detectable concentration of incoming nominations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The negative and significant coefficients for geometrically\nweighted outdegree (GWODeg) across all schools indicate a centralized structure in outgoing ties, meaning a subset\nof adolescents initiated a disproportionately high number of friendship nominations.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "For incoming ties (indegree), a\nsignificant negative coefficient was observed only in School C, indicating a centralized popularity structure in that setting.\nIn Schools A and B, indegree distributions did not deviate significantly from what would be expected by chance.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Substance use had no consistent association with sending, receiving, or matching on friendship ties. The isolated findings were more nominations sent by alcohol users in School C, alcohol homophily in School B, and weaker evidence of marijuana homophily in School B. Tobacco did not show homophily.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Substance use demonstrated no consistent or robust association with friendship dynamics in this sample.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "The only exception was a positive sender\neffect for alcohol use in School C (AME = 0.015, p < 0.05), suggesting a tendency for alcohol‑using adolescents to initiate\nmore friendship nominations in that setting.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Alcohol use showed significant homophily in School B (AME = 0.0039, p < 0.01) but not in the other schools or for other\nsubstances. Marijuana use exhibited homophily in School B (AME = 0.0020, p < 0.10), while tobacco use showed no\nhomophily in any school.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Students were consistently more likely to nominate peers of the same gender and grade. Shared sexual orientation also showed a positive association with friendship nominations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Gender and grade homophily were consistently\nsignificant across all schools (AME = 0.008–0.058, all p < 0.001), indicating that shared gender identity and grade level are\nprimary axes of friendship formation, as extensively documented in the broader adolescent literature [9,12].",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual orientation homophily was also significant and positive (AME = 0.002–0.028), an understudied pattern that suggests meaningful\nsocial stratification along lines of sexual identity even in these small, relatively homogeneous school settings.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Depression associations were negligible even when statistically detectable. AI identity affirmation showed no reliable homophily and little association with sending or receiving nominations, apart from borderline evidence for receiving more nominations in School C.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "It is important to note that depression likewise showed no substantively meaningful associations with friendship formation among AI adolescents, unlike the negative receiver effects and depression homophily commonly observed in non-AI\npopulations [46]; the isolated statistically significant estimates were negligible in magnitude (AME < 0.001).",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "AI identity was largely unassociated with sending or receiving ties,\nwith only a borderline positive receiver effect in School C.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "AI identity homophily was consistently non-significant across all schools.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The findings describe one reservation's three schools and may not extend to AI youth in racially heterogeneous schools. Comparisons with prior research are indirect because the authors could not obtain a suitable non-AI comparison sample.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we recognize that the results presented here represent\nonly three schools on one reservation. The history, policies, geography, and culture of this reservation all intersect with\nnetwork formation and maintenance patterns. Our findings may not extend to AI youth in racially heterogeneous schools,\nwhere Rees, Freng, and Winfree [17] observed different network characteristics.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "While a comparison sample of non‑AI schools\nwould strengthen generalizability, identifying a suitable dataset with comparable schools (similar size, rural location, and\ncontemporary data) was not feasible.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The data cover one stage of adolescence at one time point, so they cannot show how relationships change across school transitions or other developmental stages.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Furthermore, we acknowledge that these data reflect only one\ndevelopmental epoch of adolescents at one time point.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "a single, simultaneous observation\nof the network and of individual attributes cannot capture changes in network formation and behavior over time.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Cross-sectional ERGMs cannot distinguish adolescents selecting similar friends from adolescents becoming more similar through friendship. The study does not establish causal effects in either direction between friendships and substance use, depression, or identity.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "cross-sectional ERGMs cannot analytically distinguish selection processes—adolescents choosing friends\nwith similar attributes—from influence processes—friends becoming more similar to one another over time [4,18,48].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "formation, not as evidence that substance use, depression, or AI identity causally affect friendship formation, nor as\nevidence that friendship networks causally shape these attributes.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The demographic coefficients are descriptive benchmarks, not independent causal estimates: the authors chose the adjustment variables for interpreting the focal attributes, not for estimating every coefficient causally.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we caution against interpreting the demographic coefficients\nas independent causal effects of demographics on tie formation: the adjustment set was chosen to support the interpretation of the focal predictors, and a single adjustment set is not generally appropriate for estimating every coefficient in the\nmodel.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Peer reports used to fill in missing substance-use responses can differ from self-reports and may make friends appear more similar than they are. Excluding imputed cases left all focal effect directions and substantive conclusions intact, although some coefficients changed statistical significance.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, adolescents’ perceptions of friends’ substance use can be systematically biased, including in the direction of false consensus, in which adolescents overestimate\nfriends’ engagement in behaviors they themselves perform—and perceived friend use may diverge from friends’ own\nself-reports [34].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Second, because the peers providing the perceptions are themselves embedded in the friendship network, imputed values could in principle introduce or amplify apparent behavioral similarity among connected adolescents,\nif peer perceptions are shaped by reporters’ own behavior or by friendship-group norms.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "sensitivity\nanalyses that exclude these cases entirely leave the direction of every focal effect unchanged and reverse no substantive conclusion, although a small number of coefficients—concentrated in School B, where the excluded cases are most\nnumerous—cross conventional significance thresholds",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The identity results concern the strength of affirmation, not sorting across racial categories in a setting with little racial-category variation. They also do not rule out identity shaping kinship or family ties outside the measured school networks.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "with nearly all students identifying as AI, there is insufficient variation in racial category for identity to differentiate friendship choices.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "variation in the strength of identity\naffirmation (our measured construct) may not correspond to observable social cues that guide friendship decisions among\nadolescents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Third, kinship networks and familial relationships—which operate largely outside the school-based friendship system we\nmeasured—may be the primary arena where AI identity shapes social ties [27].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The paper offers school size, institutional environment, and local norms as possible explanations for differences among the schools, not as demonstrated mechanisms.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This\nschool‑level variation may reflect differences in school size, institutional environment, or local peer norms regarding\nstatus.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Findings indicating differences in social networks, behaviors, and friendship formation across\nthe three schools also suggest the need to investigate how community contexts influence these factors.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The intervention implications are proposals for further research, not evidence that a particular prevention program works or fails in these schools.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "common network intervention strategies aimed at reducing substance use may not be as effective as\npreviously thought [4,5,10].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "More qualitative research is needed to better understand AI adolescent networks and assess how\none can leverage existing patterns of friendship relations in interventions.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Tribe owns the data and restricts public access; the agreement also requires that the Tribe not be named in publications.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Data are not\npublicly available per our agreement with the\nTribe involved in this research, who owns the\ndata and has imposed the restriction.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Our agreement with the\nTribe and its Review Board also requires\nthat we not name the Tribe in publications.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend adolescent social network analysis to AI-majority reservation schools. They use findings from predominantly non-AI samples as benchmarks to assess which structural and individual-level patterns carry over, rather than assuming they generalize.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We examine each attribute systematically, first summarizing evidence from non-AI adolescent populations and then articulating how the cultural context of AI-majority reservation\nschools may alter these patterns.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "These benchmarks enable a structured\ninterpretation of whether observed patterns in AI-majority reservation schools align with or diverge from established\ntrends.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
